    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d118">

                <pb facs="0232" n="986" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp02v02/pg986"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 657.119/136½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the Special Representative</hi>
                        (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">House</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-11-19T13:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 19, 1917, 1 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Am distressed to differ with McCormick<anchor n="2" xml:id="d118-f1"/> but inasmuch as we are
                    fighting a war of principle I do not feel that I can consent to demand of Norway
                    what we would not in similar circumstances allow any government to demand of us,
                    namely, the cessation of exports of her own products to any place she can send
                    them. I am convinced that our only legitimate position is that we will not
                    supply the deficiencies which she thus creates for herself if the exports are to
                    our enemies.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
